    decision:
      "Unattended scheduled routine (\"continue toward a profitable and growing business, merge changes when done\"), branch claude/upbeat-dijkstra-jaze4a, picked up at origin/main tip f9d8ed0b (PR #762, dashboard-action-rate-limit-gaps-followup, merged prior session) — HEAD matched origin/main exactly at session start. Confirmed baseline green: git log confirmed HEAD === origin/main. This iteration's brief came pre-loaded with a specific, not-yet-independently-verified candidate bug rather than starting from an empty-backlog research sweep: PR #758 (renewal-charge-refund-dispute-customer-correlation) and #760 (its same-day paused-order-fix follow-up) gave charge.refunded and charge.dispute.created a Stripe-Customer-id correlation fallback for renewal-cycle charges, whose PaymentIntent is minted directly by Stripe's billing system and is never attached to a Checkout Session — but their resolution/reversal twins, refund.updated and charge.dispute.closed, were flagged as never having received the same fallback: both still resolved a Checkout Session only and simply broke (skip, no clear/mark) when the lookup missed. Independently re-verified by reading all four handlers end to end in src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts (then lines ~1635-2225) before touching anything: confirmed refund.updated's `!reversedRefundSession` branch (then ~line 2268-2273) and charge.dispute.closed's `!closedDisputeSession` branch (then ~line 1996-2000) each only console.warn+break, with zero customer-id fallback — genuinely reachable, since both closing events are correlated via the SAME payment_intent as their opened counterpart (refund.updated derives it from the refund's own `.charge`/`.payment_intent`; charge.dispute.closed from the dispute's `.payment_intent`, both via chargePaymentIntentId, identical to the opened-event derivation), so a renewal charge's missing-Checkout-Session gap applies to the closing event exactly as it does to the opening one — nothing else in the correlation chain differs. Also re-read the refund-reversal-charge-aggregate-recheck decision (this file) to confirm the fix must not regress it: refund.updated already re-fetches the parent Charge and re-checks its own amount_refunded before clearing, specifically because Stripe allows multiple independent partial refunds on one charge and a single refund sub-object's own status is not ground truth.",
    rationale:
      "Confirmed as a real, non-self-healing revenue-understatement bug: a renewal-cycle order correctly gets refundedAt/disputedAt set via the existing 'opened'-event customer-id fallback when the renewal charge is first refunded/disputed — but if that refund then reverses (an ACH/bank refund failure, the exact scenario commerce-order-refund-reversal-tracking already documents as common) or the dispute resolves won/warning_closed in the merchant's favor, the closing handler has no Checkout Session to look up (none ever existed for a renewal charge) and, before this fix, never cleared the flag. The order then stays permanently excluded from revenueTotalUsd even though Stripe genuinely returned/kept the funds with the merchant, with no recovery path short of a manual DB edit — exactly the 'opened' vs 'closed/reversed' twin gap AGENT_PROTOCOL's money/ledger integrity rules already exist to prevent (see the refund.updated/clearRefunded and commerce-order-refund-reversal-tracking entries in that same rule), reappearing on the newer renewal-charge correlation path shipped after that rule was written. Not shipping this fix would leave a live, silent, compounding headline-revenue-understatement bug for every future renewal-cycle refund reversal or won dispute — worse than the original gap PR #758 closed, because that gap was at least self-consistent (never set, never cleared); this one actively sets a flag that then gets stuck, which is strictly worse for an operator trying to trust the revenue total.\n\nShipped: gave refund.updated and charge.dispute.closed the same customer-id correlation fallback their opened twins already have. refund.updated's fallback reuses the Stripe.Charge object the handler already re-fetches for the refund-reversal-charge-aggregate-recheck aggregate amount_refunded check (no second Stripe API call) to read `.customer` directly — the aggregate recheck still gates the fallback exactly as before, so a still-in-effect different partial refund on the same charge still blocks the clear regardless of which correlation path resolved the order, preserving that invariant rather than regressing it. charge.dispute.closed retrieves the PaymentIntent for its `.customer` the same way charge.dispute.created already does (Dispute carries no `.customer` field of its own), then branches exactly like its existing Session-based path: won/warning_closed clears disputedAt (idempotent no-op if already clear), lost ensures it stays set (idempotent no-op if already set, covering a missed created event). Factored the customer-order lookup itself (getOrdersByStripeCustomerId + orderHadRecurringEntitlement — no change to the predicate, still deliberately NOT orderGrantsSoldCapabilityEntitlement per the paused-order-fix precedent) into one shared findRecurringOrderByStripeCustomerId helper in route.ts and switched all four handlers (both existing opened-event call sites too, not just the two new ones) onto it, so a future predicate correction only needs to change one place instead of drifting across up to four duplicated call sites — the exact failure mode the paused-order-fix entry above had to clean up once already. Both new failure paths reuse existing WebhookFulfillmentStep literals (bundle_order_renewal_customer_lookup, bundle_order_dispute_renewal_payment_intent_lookup, bundle_order_refund_clear, bundle_order_dispute_clear, bundle_order_dispute) rather than minting new ones, since the underlying required-write contract is identical to the opened-event steps they already gate; widened those literals' doc comments in webhook-fulfillment.ts to name the closed/reversed call sites too. Added 16 new Vitest cases across two new nested describe blocks (6 in the refund.updated suite, 10 in charge.dispute.closed's — the dispute path has both a clear AND a mark branch, doubling several case shapes), following the existing 'renewal-cycle ... via ... Customer correlation' describe blocks in the opened-event suites as the direct template: happy-path clear/mark, idempotent no-op(s), no-customer skip, no-entitled-order skip, and a 500 for each new required lookup plus the existing clear/mark-write failure. Updated src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx with a new paragraph describing the gap and the fix, placed directly after the existing renewal-charge-refund-dispute-customer-correlation / paused-order-fix writeup it extends.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run (2930 passed, 204 files, +16 over the pre-iteration 2914, matching the 16 new cases exactly) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 42-route ○/ƒ split, /api/stripe/webhook unchanged in the route list). Webhook/server-route change with no new UI surface, so route-level Vitest coverage is the bar per AGENT_PROTOCOL's own testing convention — no npm run dev/browser smoke check needed beyond that. Diff-gate anchor moves to f9d8ed0b (this iteration's own starting tip, post-PR-#762).",
