    id: "resend-webhook-bounce-complaint-suppression",
    decision:
      "Same session as the immediately-preceding archive-split entry, origin/main tip still 6d020e96 (PR #767, iterations-archive-16-split, merged earlier this session — reset the local branch onto the fresh main tip per the merged-PR-restart convention before starting new work). All five NEXT_STEPS items remain operator/legal/infra-gated and a fresh fold-scan of the newest remote branches found no live candidate, so per AGENT_PROTOCOL's empty-backlog priority order looked for a fresh sold-capability wiring gap rather than dispatching another research agent for a sixth-plus pass over the same five exhausted tiers.\n\nGrepped the CAN-SPAM/suppression surface directly (src/app/api/unsubscribe/route.ts, src/lib/db.ts's suppressEmail/getSuppressedEmails, convex/suppressions.ts) and found the suppression list has exactly one write path: the operator-visible unsubscribe click. There is no Resend delivery-event webhook anywhere in the repo (confirmed via `find src/app/api -maxdepth 2 -type d` — no resend/ directory existed) — a hard bounce (invalid/nonexistent mailbox) or a spam complaint left that address able to receive outreach/receipts/marketing packs/DNS handoff email forever, with zero operator signal and a real, compounding sender-reputation cost: repeatedly mailing a hard-bounced or complained address is exactly what gets a sending domain's future mail (including transactional receipts) filtered or blocked by inbox providers. This is a genuinely fresh gap, not a re-tread — grepped the full ITERATIONS/BUSINESS_DECISIONS history for 'bounce'/'svix'/'complain' first and found only one unrelated prior mention (a CRM-reply-loop bounce-visibility fix, contact-form-reply-bounce-visibility, nothing to do with Resend's own delivery webhooks).",
    rationale:
      "Shipped a new POST /api/resend/webhook route, dormant (returns 501) until an operator sets RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET — no ALLOW_* feature flag needed, since an unset secret alone makes the route refuse to process anything, matching the commerce-payouts-stripe-connect-express-account-scaffold precedent of shipping the risk-free half of a feature ungated while the money/reputation-sensitive half stays behind an explicit switch (here, the switch is simply 'is the secret configured').\n\nsrc/lib/email/resend-webhook.ts (pure, fully unit-tested) implements Resend's Svix-scheme signature verification by hand rather than adding the svix npm package as a new dependency — this repo already sends via raw fetch to Resend's API (no `resend` SDK dependency either) and hand-rolls HMAC-SHA256 + timingSafeEqual for its two other signed-secret surfaces (src/lib/auth/operator.ts's passwordsMatch, src/lib/email/unsubscribe-token.ts), so a third hand-rolled instance keeps the pattern consistent rather than introducing a new dependency for one endpoint. verifyResendWebhookSignature takes an injectable nowSeconds (never calls Date.now() internally) so every test case is deterministic, mirroring checkRateLimit's own injectable-now discipline; it hard-rejects an over-long svix-id/svix-signature header before any Buffer.from/HMAC work (same opaque-token size-bound class as MAX_UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_LENGTH), enforces Svix's documented 5-minute timestamp tolerance window against replay, and walks every space-separated `v1,<base64>` candidate in svix-signature (Svix allows multiple concurrent signing keys) via timingSafeEqual rather than a raw `===`.\n\nisPermanentBounceEvent suppresses on `email.bounced` unless the payload carries an explicit non-Permanent `bounce.type` (Transient/Undetermined) — a missing bounce.type is treated as permanent too, since Resend's own docs describe this event as firing only when the recipient's mail server permanently rejected the message (confirmed via WebSearch against Resend's docs and the resend-skills reference repo, since a direct WebFetch to resend.com's own docs pages 403'd from this sandbox); this conservative default only under-suppresses if a future Resend payload variant reuses email.bounced for a soft failure, at which point the explicit non-Permanent escape hatch already protects against it. isComplaintEvent (email.complained) always suppresses — no sub-type to weigh, since a spam complaint is unconditionally actionable regardless of provenance.\n\nThe route mirrors the Stripe webhook's own stripe-webhook-rate-limit-post-verify precedent exactly: signature verification runs before checkRateLimit, because clientIpFromHeaders derives its key from a spoofable x-forwarded-for/x-real-ip header, and a pre-verification IP-keyed gate would let an attacker impersonate Resend's sending IPs, exhaust the bucket with garbage POSTs that fail verification, then have Resend's own real redeliveries from that IP land in the same already-exhausted bucket. RESEND_WEBHOOK_SUPPRESSION_CONVEX_PRIMARY (src/lib/email/suppression-persist.ts, requireConvex: true, same shape as the pre-existing UNSUBSCRIBE_CONVEX_PRIMARY / OPERATOR_UNSUPPRESS_CONVEX_PRIMARY constants) fails the write closed on a Convex mutation failure — isEmailSuppressed reads Convex-first, so a silent SQLite-only fallback here would let the handler return 200 (Resend stops retrying) while other instances kept sending to an address that just hard-bounced or complained; a required-write failure instead returns 500 so Resend/Svix's own backoff-retry contract (the same one Stripe's webhook relies on) actually recovers the suppression. Every other Resend event type (delivered, opened, clicked, delivery_delayed, …) is acknowledged with 200 and ignored, matching the Stripe webhook's own default:-break-then-200 pattern for unhandled event types.\n\nDocumented in .env.example (RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET, with the exact Resend-dashboard setup steps), /docs/launch-checklist (a new step in section 3, Outreach, right after the SPF/DKIM/DMARC step — same deliverability-protection grouping) and /docs/architecture (a new paragraph in the CAN-SPAM/suppression section describing the signature scheme, the bounce/complaint classification rule, and the fail-closed write contract). No existing suppression-list consumer (isEmailSuppressed, the dashboard's SuppressedEmailsPanel, every sendEmail call site's pre-send check) needed any change — this only adds a new writer to a list every send path already reads Convex-first.\n\nAdded src/lib/email/resend-webhook.test.ts (29 cases: signature accept/reject across tampered body, wrong secret, mismatched id, stale/future/boundary timestamp, missing headers, over-long id/signature, malformed base64, multi-candidate signature header; bounce/complaint classification across Permanent/Transient/Undetermined/absent bounce.type; recipient extraction from array/string/missing/blank `to`) and src/app/api/resend/webhook/route.test.ts (11 cases: 501 when unconfigured, 400 on invalid signature, 400 on invalid JSON, suppress on permanent bounce / missing-bounce-type / complaint, no-suppress on Transient bounce / unrelated event type / missing recipient, 500-with-received:false on a suppression write failure, and a 120-request rate-limit boundary test). Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run (3003 passed, up from 2963 — 40 new, 207 files) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, 43-route ○/ƒ split — one new ƒ /api/resend/webhook route) + an npm run dev smoke check confirming /docs/launch-checklist and /docs/architecture both render the new documentation, POST /api/resend/webhook returns 501 with RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET unset, and /dashboard/leads + /preview/example-bakery both still 200; dev server killed, port 3100 confirmed freed, local.db deleted afterward. NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes a real, previously-unaudited sender-reputation/deliverability gap, not any of the five gated items. Diff-gate anchor moves to 6d020e96 (this session's own starting tip after merging PR #767).",
