      "Unattended scheduled routine, branch claude/nice-euler-3gkv3c. Session started at origin/main tip e8982f3f (PR #770, daily-digest-bounce-complaint-footnote, merged prior session) — HEAD matched origin/main exactly. mcp__github__list_pull_requests reconfirmed the sole open PR is still #202 (unrelated, weeks-stale human draft). A git fetch of every remote branch plus a git rev-list ahead-count / git diff --stat spot-check of the newest ~13 sibling branches by commit time found each one's diff matching an already-merged, already-ITERATIONS-logged PR (owners traced 1:1 to entries already in the ITERATIONS array, e.g. claude/nice-euler-r5775w -> account-updated-connect-status-refresh, claude/upbeat-dijkstra-8wbrm0 -> daily-digest-bounce-complaint-footnote) — no live fold candidate. All five NEXT_STEPS items reconfirmed operator/legal/infra-gated by re-reading the array directly.\n\nA direct (non-delegated) empty-backlog audit — reading convex/orders.ts and convex/commerceOrders.ts's replace()-based clear* mutations for the field-omission landmine AGENT_PROTOCOL warns about, and the freshly-added convex/suppressions.ts reason field — found those already correct (every conditional-spread replace() call already carries lifetimeRevenueUsd/lastRenewalInvoiceId/quantity/customerEmail forward; suppressions.suppress's existing-row early-return already makes reason idempotent by design). The genuine gap: src/app/api/resend/webhook/route.ts (the bounce/complaint suppression webhook shipped two iterations earlier, PR #768) read the entire raw request body via `await request.text()` with no byte ceiling, before doing anything else including signature verification — a public, unauthenticated POST route once RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET is set, no additional gate. Every other public POST route in this codebase (contact form, checkout, preview-open, unsubscribe, marketing/generate) reads its body through readJsonBodyWithByteLimit / readFormBodyWithByteLimit (src/lib/security/json-body.ts), which streams and cancels once the running total exceeds MAX_PUBLIC_JSON_BODY_BYTES (32 KiB) — json-body.ts's own header comment documents exactly one exception, the Stripe webhook, because Stripe signs a payload that can legitimately exceed the ceiling. That exception does not extend to Resend: Resend delivery-event payloads are small, fixed-shape JSON (event type, recipient, bounce sub-type), nowhere near 32 KiB, so this route appears to have mechanically copied the Stripe webhook's rate-limit-after-verify pattern (correctly — clientIp is attacker-controlled pre-verification, so rate-limiting before signature check would let a spoofed IP burn out the real sender's bucket) but also copied its unbounded-body read without the justification that makes it safe for Stripe specifically. A scripted caller (with or without a truthful Content-Length) could force unbounded memory allocation on every request, before signature verification ever runs.",
    rationale:
      "Added readTextBodyWithByteLimit to src/lib/security/json-body.ts — a new sibling to readJsonBodyWithByteLimit/readFormBodyWithByteLimit that shares the same underlying readBoundedArrayBuffer streaming-bounded-read (Content-Length pre-check, then cancel the stream once the running total exceeds maxBytes) but returns the decoded raw text unparsed, since a signature-verifying webhook needs the exact raw bytes before any JSON.parse. Defaults to the shared MAX_PUBLIC_JSON_BODY_BYTES (32 KiB) ceiling, same as every other public route in this class.\n\nsrc/app/api/resend/webhook/route.ts: swapped `await request.text()` for `await readTextBodyWithByteLimit(request, MAX_PUBLIC_JSON_BODY_BYTES)`, returning 413 'Request body too large' on overflow before signature verification runs. This is deliberately before verification, not after (unlike the rate-limit ordering, which is intentionally post-verification for the IP-spoofing reason above): an over-sized body can never match a legitimate Resend signature anyway (Resend never sends a payload anywhere near this large), so gating the byte ceiling pre-verification creates no oracle an attacker could act on — it's a pure resource-exhaustion guard, not a business-logic branch. Rejecting a request this size can only ever affect malformed/malicious/misconfigured traffic, never a real Resend delivery event.\n\nUpdated src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx's existing public-JSON-body-byte-ceiling paragraph to note the Resend webhook is not exempt the way Stripe's is, and now shares the same ceiling via readTextBodyWithByteLimit.\n\nTests: src/lib/security/json-body.test.ts — 6 new readTextBodyWithByteLimit cases (returns raw text unmodified, rejects on over-Content-Length, rejects on over-limit body with no Content-Length, accepts a body exactly at the max, respects a custom maxBytes override, returns an empty string for an empty body) mirroring the existing readJsonBodyWithByteLimit test shapes. src/app/api/resend/webhook/route.test.ts — one new case: an oversized body (with a deliberately-wrong signature, since a body this large is rejected before verification runs) returns 413 and never calls suppressEmail.\n\nVerified: npm ci (409 packages, 9 known high-severity findings, unchanged dev-only) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run (3029 passed, +7 over the pre-iteration 3022 baseline, 208 files, same file count) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 43-route ○/ƒ split, /api/resend/webhook still listed as ƒ). NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes a real, previously-unaudited resource-exhaustion gap on a webhook shipped two iterations earlier, not any of the five gated items. Diff-gate anchor moves to e8982f3f (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#770).",
