    rationale:
      "Root cause (confirmed via a debug-instrumented Playwright run capturing document.activeElement + the anchor button's live `disabled` attribute across several hundred ms): every dashboard-focus-restore anchor (leadNotesToggleElementId / leadCommerceToggleElementId / inquiryReplyToggleElementId) is itself `disabled={isPending}`, same as every row action button. React's useTransition keeps `isPending` true for the WHOLE duration of an async `startTransition(async () => ...)` callback, not just its synchronous portion — so a same-tick `document.getElementById(anchorId)?.focus()` call placed at the end of that callback (right after the success-path setState calls, before the callback returns) runs while the anchor button is still disabled and therefore unfocusable; the browser silently drops the focus() call and leaves focus on <body> instead. This is invisible to curl/tsc/lint/vitest (none render a real DOM or execute a real focus() call) and only reproduces under a real browser with `disabled={isPending}` genuinely true at focus-time. A direct (non-transition) close, e.g. clicking Cancel, has no such race since isPending is already false when it fires — which is why the bug had gone unnoticed: every panel's Cancel path worked fine, and nobody had live-browser-verified the Save/Send-reply path specifically.\n\nFix: leads-client.tsx now routes every toggle-button-target refocus through a queued mechanism instead of a same-tick .focus() call. `pendingFocusElementIdRef` (a ref, not state, so the effect can clear it without a react-hooks/set-state-in-effect lint violation) holds the pending anchor id; `focusToken` (a monotonic counter, deliberately not the id itself, so re-queuing the SAME anchor id twice in a row — e.g. snooze, unsnooze, snooze again — still re-triggers the effect; a repeated identical state value would otherwise bail out of the dependency-change check and silently skip a later focus) triggers a `useEffect([isPending, focusToken])` that focuses the ref'd id and clears it as soon as `isPending` is — or becomes — false, whichever happens first. `queueFocusElement(id)` replaces every same-tick `document.getElementById(id)?.focus()` call whose target is one of the three toggle-button anchors: closeNotesEditor, closeCommerceEditor, closeReplyComposer (all three pre-existing, now-fixed), plus the three new main-table handlers (handleSnooze/handleClearSnooze's and handleColdDisposition's non-headingId branch, and handleCreateStripeConnectAccount). Deliberately left unchanged: every headingId-target `.focus()` call (due-queue panel headings, PRODUCTION_DEPLOY_HANDOFFS_HEADING_ID and its dozen siblings) — those targets are `<h2>`/`<h3>` elements with `tabIndex={-1}`, never `disabled`, so they were never subject to this race and don't need the queued mechanism.\n\nVerified via a real Playwright browser (globally-installed Chromium at /opt/pw-browsers, PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH set, repo has no playwright devDependency — same setup as contact-form-success-message-focus-restore) against a running `npm run dev` instance: confirmed via document.activeElement that (1) Save-notes' pre-existing closeNotesEditor race is fixed (previously reproduced BODY as document.activeElement after Save; now lands on the Notes toggle button), (2) the new Snooze→Unsnooze→Snooze-again main-table sequence correctly restores focus to the Notes toggle on every step including repeated identical anchor ids, confirmed both via the rendered DOM state and (temporarily, removed before commit) debug instrumentation showing the effect firing exactly as designed. A debug run also showed the effect's fire can trail the DOM's own due-queue-swap update by up to roughly a second under rapid back-to-back scripted actions (an artifact of how long `isPending` stays true across a real network round trip) — bounded and eventually-consistent, never permanently lost, and not something a real human operator clicking at normal speed would perceive; a scripted 200-300ms assertion window in my own verification script was too tight for this and needed lengthening to 1s, which is a test-harness calibration note, not an application defect. Commerce-editor Save and Stripe Connect create-account paths share the identical `queueFocusElement`/effect mechanism already proven correct for the notes/snooze cases above, so they were verified by code-path identity rather than separately reproducing eligibility state (Commerce catalog eligibility needs specific stage/order preconditions the demo seed data doesn't include) — every call site funnels through the same single effect, so there is no per-handler branch that could behave differently.\n\nTests: no new pure-function unit tests were added — per this repo's own client-component-testing convention (React client components have no test harness; extract pure logic instead), the fix reuses the ALREADY-tested leadNotesToggleElementId/leadCommerceToggleElementId id builders (dashboard-focus-restore.test.ts) with no new pure logic to extract; the actual regression (a live DOM disabled/focus interaction under React transition timing) is not expressible as a Vitest unit test and was verified with a real browser instead, matching how contact-form-success-message-focus-restore was itself verified.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean, including the react-hooks/set-state-in-effect rule that caught and required the ref+token redesign over a naive setState-in-effect first draft) + npx vitest run (3043 passed, unchanged from the pre-iteration baseline — no new pure logic to cover, no regression) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 43-route ○/ƒ split) + the live Playwright browser verification described above (dev server killed, port 3100 confirmed freed, local.db deleted afterward). NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes a genuine, previously-undetected accessibility regression spanning three already-shipped focus-restore call sites plus three newly-identified ones, not any of the five gated items. Diff-gate anchor moves to 966e650 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#773).",
