      "Unattended scheduled routine (\"continue toward a profitable and growing business, merge changes when done\"), branch claude/upbeat-dijkstra-wh2whh, picked up at origin/main tip 0c47e908 (PR #775, buffer-schedule-result-summary-live-region, merged prior session) — HEAD matched origin/main exactly at session start. Confirmed baseline green: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run (3043 passed, 208 files) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 43-route split). mcp__github__list_pull_requests reconfirmed the sole open PR is still #202 (unrelated, weeks-stale human draft). A `git fetch origin '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*' --prune` sweep (695 remote heads) of the newest ~20 claude/* branches by push time found every one diffed empty against origin/main for src/convex/.github-workflows paths — content already merged (PRs up to #775) — no live fold candidate. Diff-gate (`git log --oneline 966e6501..HEAD -- src convex .github/workflows`, excluding prompt_process.tsx) showed only the two already-logged commits from the immediately-preceding two sessions (buffer-schedule-result-summary-live-region, dashboard-focus-restore-async-transition-race), so per the waste-reduction rule dispatched one research agent instead of a full five-tier sweep, steered at the Convex/SQLite parity tier plus the newest Resend-webhook/suppression-reason code (already the subject of three prior follow-up fixes, making a fourth plausible) and every Stripe webhook lifecycle-pair. Independently re-verified every candidate the agent ruled out by reading the cited source directly before accepting the report (Stripe Customer-id correlation ambiguity across multiple entitled orders — ruled out after confirming no code path anywhere reuses an existing Stripe Customer id at checkout creation, so a customerId maps 1:1 to at most one order in practice; the Resend webhook's 120/60s per-IP rate limit — confirmed non-lossy, since a 429 carries Retry-After and Resend/Svix redelivers with backoff, identical to the accepted Stripe-webhook-rate-limit-post-verify precedent; TOCTOU on resendBundleBuyerEmailAction's pre-send handoff.status snapshot — confirmed already safe, since updateDeployHandoffStatus applies preferAdvancedHandoffStatus never-downgrade inside the write on both backends, not just at the call site).\n\nThe one genuine, previously-unaudited gap, independently reproduced by reading convex/suppressions.ts, src/lib/email/suppression.ts, and src/lib/db.ts's SQLite fallback directly: convex/suppressions.ts's `suppress` mutation computed `preferred = preferSuppressionReason(existing.reason, args.reason)` — which internally defaults `existing.reason` via `suppressionReasonOrDefault` (undefined -> \"unsubscribe\") before ranking — but then compared that defaulted `preferred` value against the RAW, undefaulted `existing.reason` (`if (preferred !== existing.reason)`). For any row written before the `reason` column existed (schema.ts declares it `v.optional` specifically for this backward-compat case, so a real legacy row has `existing.reason === undefined`), any repeat `suppress()` call — even an ordinary same-category unsubscribe-again — computed `preferred = \"unsubscribe\"`, and `\"unsubscribe\" !== undefined` is always true, so the mutation patched the row and stamped a fresh `reasonUpdatedAt` on every single repeat call, never converging to a true no-op. The SQLite fallback does not share this bug: `suppression_list.reason` is `NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unsubscribe'`, so its `existing.reason` is never undefined for the same legacy-row scenario, and the equivalent `preferred !== existing.reason` comparison there correctly no-ops. Same input (a repeat suppress call for a legacy row), different backend, different (non-idempotent vs idempotent) behavior — the exact Convex/SQLite parity bug class AGENT_PROTOCOL's dual-backend invariants section targets. Severity is capped, not a data-integrity risk: the row's `reason` still resolves to \"unsubscribe\" either way (via `suppressionReasonOrDefault` at every read site), so it stays correctly excluded from the bounce/complaint reputation metrics (`isBounceOrComplaintSuppression` filters first, before `suppressionRecencyMs` is ever read) — the bug is an unnecessary non-idempotent write plus a fabricated `reasonUpdatedAt` on legacy rows, not a wrong classification.",
    rationale:
      "Fixed by computing `existingReason = suppressionReasonOrDefault(existing.reason)` first and comparing `preferred !== existingReason` (both sides now defaulted the same way) instead of the mismatched `preferred !== existing.reason` (defaulted vs raw). Imported `suppressionReasonOrDefault` alongside the already-imported `preferSuppressionReason` from `../src/lib/email/suppression` — no new module, no schema change, one-line comparison fix plus a doc comment explaining why the two sides must both be defaulted. Two new convex/suppressions.test.ts cases seed a genuine legacy row directly via `t.run(async (ctx) => ctx.db.insert(\"suppressions\", { email }))` (bypassing the `suppress` mutation entirely, since every call through it always sets a reason — the only way to produce a real `reason === undefined` row in a test is a direct insert, mirroring the existing checkoutSessions.test.ts precedent for simulating a stray/legacy row shape): one confirms a same-category repeat call is now a true no-op (raw `reason` and `reasonUpdatedAt` both stay unset — read via `ctx.db.get`, not the `list` query, since Convex strips undefined-valued keys from a query's returned object entirely and `toEqual`/`objectContaining` treat an explicit `undefined` differently from a missing key); the other confirms a legacy row still correctly upgrades on a genuine bounce/complaint. Updated the CAN-SPAM/suppression paragraph in src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx to describe the fix and the Convex/SQLite asymmetry that caused it. Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run (up from 3043, all new suppressions cases passing, no regressions) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 43-route split). This is a pure Convex-mutation/data-layer fix with no new UI surface, so the Vitest coverage above is the bar per AGENT_PROTOCOL's own testing convention — no npm run dev/browser smoke check needed beyond that. NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes a real Convex/SQLite dual-backend parity gap in code shipped several iterations earlier, not any of the five gated items. Diff-gate anchor moves to 0c47e908 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#775).",
